+$$ \text{HBE} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i = 1}^{n}\sum_{j = 1}^{p} (x_{ij} - \widehat{x}_{ij})^2}$${#eq-equation1}
+
+We can consider $e_i = \sqrt{\sum_{j = 1}^{p} (x_{ij} - \widehat{x}_{ij})^2}$ to be the residual for each observation. @fig-p-d-error-in-2d-two-curvy visualizes these residuals as a density (a), as colors in the NLDR layout (b), and in a tour (c). Larger residuals are concentrated in one cluster, reflecting the intentional design that this cluster has an intrinsic higher-dimensional structure (approximately \tD{}) and therefore cannot be fully represented in a \gD{} embedding. Importantly, HBE is not intended to replace existing embedding-quality metrics. Instead, it provides a complementary diagnostic perspective, highlighting where the embedding fails to represent the original data structure in \pD{}. Differences between HBE and other metrics can therefore be interpreted as informative signals about trade-offs between local and global structure preservation.
