+## Choosing from a selection of layouts {#sec-bestfit}
+<!--updated according to the reviews-->
 
-<!-- Does this really need an entire subsection? It's only 3 sentences long. -->
+@fig-toy-rmse shows the diagnostic plot of HBE vs binwidth (i) for a selection of plots from which to use. Color in all plots indicates the layout (a-f). The details of how they were generated are in the Appendix. There is nothing special about this selection of layouts other than that all five methods were used: (a, f) tSNE, (b) UMAP, (c) PHATE, (d) TriMAP, (e) PaCMAP. They represent different patterns, but all agree on there being two clusters, with varying distance and shape between them. The process to make a decision is:
 
-NLDR methods are primarily designed for visualization and exploration rather than reconstruction, and do not explicitly provide out-of-sample prediction. Of the five methods studied here, only UMAP provides a `predict()` function for embedding new data points based on the learned manifold [@tomasz2023]. Several other approaches, not used here, PCA, neural network autoencoders [@hinton2006] and parametric tSNE [@van2009] support prediction. 
+1. Fit the model to a range of binwidths for each layout, calculating HBE.
+2. Plot each HBE against binwidth, for all layouts. 
+3. The better representation(s) will have universally lower HBE. When the lines cross, one needs to decide whether the local structure (smaller binwidth) is more important than the larger structure (large binwidth).
+4. Examine the model for the chosen layout in \pD{}. The exact choice of binwidth isn't important, but we recommend a reasonably small one, so that the wireframe is smoother, and there is a chance to see small details of departures between the fitted model and the data. (See @fig-best-fit-tsne b, c for this data.)
 
-A benefit of our approach is that for any NLDR method, it provides a way to predict the layout position of a new observation, $x'$. The steps are (1) determine the closest bin centroid in \pD{}, $C^{(p)}_{h}$ and (2) predict the embedding to be the bin centroid in \gD{}, $C^{(2)}_{h}$. 
+For this set of layouts, either a or f would be preferable to the other layouts, because the HBE values are lower at every binwidth. (Note that a was used to illustrate the approach in the previous sections.) 
 
-### Tuning
+Using binwidth as the horizontal axis is a choice. An alternative is to use the average bin count. In theory, this is the more sensible choice. because it summarizes the grid occupancy of the data. Using binwidth has the problem that a smaller binwidth for one layout might be equivalent to a larger binwidth in a different layout, as a fraction of the observations. Using binwidth sets up a possible mismatching HBE value. However, using average bin count tends to lose the ability to penalize layouts that exaggerate inter-cluster distance (see plots in the Appendix). An argument for why binwidth is the better choice is that it treats the \gD{} space as a uniform canvas on which to compare the representations.
 
-<!-- The model fitting is based on these parameters:  -->
+It can also be useful to calculate the suite of other metrics to measure the fit, for each layout, which is done in plot ii. The comparison with HBE requires that a single value be chosen. Here, $a_1=0.05$ is used, but the actual choice wouldn't substantially matter because the ordering of the curves is quite consistent across binwidths. Note that in this parallel coordinate plot, the individual metrics are individually scaled to range between 0 and 1.  HBE shows most agreement with rARNX, both rank a and f the best. They differ in how they rate (1) layout d - HBE would consider it the worst, and rARNX to be not quite as good as a and f - and (2) layout c - rARNX considers it to be the worst, and HBE in the middle. The other three metrics roughly agree with each other and are almost the polar opposite of HBE. This is most likely explained by the tension between local and global structure. Most will prefer a bigger distance between clusters in the layout, or at least not penalise this. The problem is that this leads to incorrect perception when the clusters are not widely separated, as is the case for this data (@fig-best-fit-tsne). 
 
-<!-- - hexagon bin parameters -->
-<!--     - bottom left bin position $(s_1, \ s_2)$,  -->
-<!--     - the number of bins in the horizontal direction ($b_1$), which controls the number of bins the vertical direction ($b_2$), total number of bins ($b$), and total number of non-empty bins ($m$). -->
-<!-- - low count bin removal using standardized bin counts ($w_h = n_h/n, ~~h=1, \dots m$). -->
+The final check is to examine the chosen model fit (or several top-rated fits) in the \pD{} data space @fig-best-fit-tsne (b, c). As with all model fitting, if the data closely hugs the model representation, in all projections shown by the tour, then the choice is further supported. This is a suitable approach to better understand why metrics disagree on layouts. The Appendix has a comparison of the model fits for layouts a and c. Layout c twists extremely along the 2- and \tD{} manifolds where the data lies. Twisting in the fitted model appears to be very common for all methods for many data sets, which one could imagine as a solution for squashing higher dimensions into lower ones while preserving the neighborhood. In this case, it is quite extreme. 
 
-The model fitting is based on several parameters, including the hexagon bin parameters and the low count bin removal process. The hexagon bin parameters define the bottom-left bin position $(s_1, \ s_2)$, the number of bins in the horizontal direction ($b_1$), which also determines the number of bins in the vertical direction ($b_2$), the total number of bins ($b$), and the total number of non-empty bins ($m$). Low count bins are removed using standardized bin counts, defined as $w_h = n_h/n, ~~h=1, \dots m$.
-
-Default values are provided for each of these, but deciding on the best model fit is assisted by examining a range of values. The default number of bins $b=b_1\times b_2$ is computed based on the sample size, by setting $b_1=n^{1/3}$, consistent with the Diaconis-Freedman rule [@freedman1981]. The value of $b_2$ is determined analytically by $b_1, q, r_2$ (@eq-bin2). Values of $b_1$ between $2$ and $b_1 = \sqrt{n/r_2}$ are recommended, where the dependence on $r_2$ reflects the preservation of aspect ratio in the NLDR layout.
-
-@fig-bins-two-curvy shows the hexbin grids for three choices of $b_1$. While the number of bins is the common parameter to modify, bin start positions $(s_1, \ s_2)$ can be worth experimenting with also because  it can also change bin counts. 
