+<!--NLDR methods focus on a visualization solution for exploration without the ability to place new observations in a layout. Partially, this is also due to the methods being designed to operate only on the interpoint distance matrices instead of $\pD{}$ data.--> Only UMAP provides a way to predict the layout positions of new observations [@tomasz2023] when the full data is available, and this is made available in the `umap` [@leland2018] software. Other approaches for embedding new data include neural network autoencoders [@hinton2006] and parametric tSNE [@van2009] but they do not directly relate to the common methods presented here.  
+
+A benefit of our approach is that with the model representation, we can predict layout positions for any method. To illustrate this, we will first compare the approach with the predictions provided by UMAP, by generating a test set of `2NC7`. We will also show predictions of this test set for the layouts used in @fig-toy-rmse.
+
+Our procedure for the predicting the layout position of a new observation is: (i) identify the closest bin centroid in \pD{}, $\mathbfit{c}^{(p)}_{h*}$, and (ii) assign the corresponding centroid in \gD{}, $\mathbfit{c}^{(2)}_{h*}$, as the predicted embedding. This is not entirely different from how UMAP provides predictions, which is to find the nearest neighbors in \pD{}, and interpolate their positions in \gD{} to provide the predicted embedding. This can be considered to be an estimate of the mapping $g$ that maps the new observation ($\mathbfit{x}^*$) into the layout ($\mathbfit{y}^*$), $\mathbfit{y}^*  = \widehat{g}(\mathbfit{x}^*), \widehat{g}\text{:}~ \mathbb{R}^p \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^2$.
+
+@fig-prediction-comparison shows the results for UMAP (a) and our predictions (b). The `2NC7` data is divided into training and test sets, using a 70% split. The training data is used to make the \gD{} layout. (This is similar but not quite the same as the layout on the full data seen in @fig-toy-rmse plot b.) The test set is held out for prediction. In both cases, it can be seen that the test set matches the training set in the layout quite well. The main differences are that our predictions are to the bin centroid, so it looks gridded. Adding some jitter to the predicted values would make them look more similar to the UMAP predictions. Just because we can, doesn't mean we should - for this data, the method provides demonstrably reasonable results, and better than having no method at all. 
+
+## Applications {#sec-applications}
+
+To illustrate the approach, we use two examples: PBMC3k data (single cell gene expression), where an NLDR layout is used to represent cluster structure present in the \pD{} data, and MNIST hand-written digits, where NLDR is used to represent a low-dimensional nonlinear manifold in \pD{}.
+
+### PBMC3k {#sec-pbmc}
+
